<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="d6e50e54" lines="8-25">
<description>
Move each maroon marker toward the blue rectangle and recolor the rectangle red.

Input structure
A background canvas contains one solid blue rectangular object and several isolated
maroon marker pixels. Each marker is horizontally or vertically aligned with the
rectangle and lies outside it.

Rule
Start with a background canvas, repaint the blue rectangle red, and move each
maroon marker straight toward the rectangle along its row or column. Exactly one
marker is strictly closer to the rectangle than all others and enters the
rectangle boundary; every other marker lands in the adjacent exterior cell just
outside its aligned side.

Key insight
The single entering marker is the unique global nearest marker to the rectangle,
not the nearest marker on each of the four sides.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="edcc2ff0" lines="47-63">
<description>
Keep only the lower-panel colors named by the top markers and expand each marker into an object-count bar.

Input structure
A black instruction strip sits above a non-black rectangular scene panel.  The strip contains one-cell markers
in the first column, and each marker color is a candidate object color in the lower panel.  The lower panel has
a unique dominant background color and scattered 4-connected foreground objects.

Rule
In the lower panel, preserve objects whose colors appear as top markers and recolor every other foreground
object to the panel background.  In the instruction strip, replace each marker by a horizontal run from column 0
whose length is the number of 4-connected lower-panel objects of that marker color; erase markers with count 0.

Key insight
The top strip is a color filter plus a compact count summary: marker colors select which lower-panel objects
survive, and their bars report the number of surviving objects by color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="a09f6c25" lines="33-50">
<description>
Recolor each non-singleton red object by its symmetry axis and erase isolated red pixels.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color and red 4-connected foreground objects.
- Every non-singleton red object has exactly one reflection symmetry category:
  horizontal, vertical, or diagonal.
- Single red pixels are noise.

Rule:
- Start from an all-background output of the same shape.
- Recolor horizontally symmetric red objects blue, vertically symmetric objects
  green, and diagonally symmetric objects pink.
- Leave isolated red pixels as background.

Key insight: The target color is not tied to object position or size; it is a
label for the object's reflection symmetry.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="3a25b0d8" lines="9-22">
<description>
Fill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.

Input structure:
- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.
- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.

Rule:
- Find the two 8-connected objects.
- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).
- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.

Key insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.
</description>
